# Target C: a coupled Delaunay transfer for arbitrary convex containers

Date: 2026-10-07. Authority: audit.77, main
`d71085cd0130941642796d8717d7e9fb2949b718`.
**Proved candidate awaiting independent audit.** No promotion.

## 1. The transfer and its exact remaining endpoint

Let A be any finite planar unit-separated source. Extend **the complete A**
to one saturated auxiliary packing P of the plane. Use one Delaunay
triangulation of P for every container. Let C be any compact convex polygon
with nonempty interior, area Q, perimeter p, and z=|A intersect C|, with
boundary source labels included. Write a=sqrt(3)/4 and a_0=2a, and put

`S=Q/a_0+p/2+1-z>=0`,
`B=|{x in P intersect C:dist(x,partial C)<=2}|`.

There are at most floor(S) auxiliary additions in C, and

`B <= 20*p/pi+25`.

Let T be every Delaunay triangle fully contained in C and put
`Sigma=sum_(t in T)(area(t)-a)`. Then

`0<=Sigma<=a_0*S+28*a*B`.

Apart from a set of at most

`73*B+(12/delta+450)*Sigma` vertices,
`delta=(2-sqrt(3))/4`,

every interior vertex farther than two from the boundary has six incident
triangles, all acute with individual excess below 1/100. These triangles
have side/angle bounds

`L-1<=5*(area(t)-a)`,
`|angle-pi/3|<=50*(area(t)-a)`.

All counts include original source points and auxiliary additions; the
construction never deletes a contact isolate. Boundary strips, nonunit
closing chords and different containers use the **same** auxiliary packing,
so their rows and counts are geometrically compatible. Overlapping
containers may not spend their shared triangle excess twice.

This removes the unit-wall/regular-profile hypothesis from the geometric
transfer. In the canonical macroscopic chamber or exterior-cap branch,
S=O(D), p=O(D), z=Omega(D^2): the completion and exceptional skeleton cost
O(D), while the original quadratic population remains. It supplies an
actual coupled near-triangular skeleton for arbitrary convex containers,
not merely an area estimate or an independently prescribed lattice.

`arbitrary canonical container -> one coupled completion with O(D) cost
and actual Delaunay defect skeleton -> source-realized movement/payment
of this skeleton -> audit.64 contact/diameter exchange`.

Only the first implication is proved. Neither auxiliary additions nor
triangulation edges are automatically an admissible packet or positive
contacts. The O(D) boundary/metric cost must be paid in the exact finite
ledger; no one-integer contradiction drops it.

## 2. Saturation, boundary and degree counts

Enumerate a dense countable subset of the plane and add a proposed point
whenever it is unit-separated from the current packing. Its locally finite
limit P contains all of A and covers the plane by closed unit disks. If
some x had distance greater than one from P, a sufficiently close proposed
point would have been admitted, a contradiction. Bounded Voronoi cells
and their convex cyclic dual faces give a Delaunay tiling; triangulate each
co-circular face. Every circumradius is at most one and every edge length
is between one and two. No new literature statement beyond this elementary
empty-circle construction is imported.

Oler applies to P intersect C, since it is finite. Its size is at most
Q/a_0+p/2+1, hence the number of points added in C is at most floor(S).
This counts all old boundary labels, including chord/side exceptions.

The disjoint open radius-1/2 disks about the B boundary-strip points lie
in the radius-5/2 neighbourhood of partial C. For any convex C and s>=0,
the area of that neighbourhood is at most 2sp+pi*s^2: outer parallel
area is Q+sp+pi*s^2; the lost inner strip has area at most sp, by
integrating the perimeters of the convex inner parallel sets, each at
most p. This remains valid when the inner parallel set is empty.
Set s=5/2 and divide by pi/4 to obtain B<=20p/pi+25.

At a vertex, every Delaunay neighbour is at distance at most two. The
radius-1/2 disjoint disks about that vertex and all its neighbours lie
in a radius-5/2 disk. Thus degree+1<=25 and degree<=24. The number of
incident triangles is also at most 24. These rough constants are uniform
and require no generic position, degree-six assumption or contact graph.

## 3. Bulk Euler accounting without assuming a disk

The union of the contained triangles is a finite pure planar simplicial
complex. It can have holes or meet at isolated common vertices. Let V,E,T
be its vertex, edge and triangle counts, b its number of edges incident
to only one triangle, and chi its Euler characteristic. Then

`3*T=2*E-b`, `chi=V-E+T`, hence `T=2*V-b-2*chi`.

If c is its number of connected components, chi<=c. All saturated-packing
points in C with boundary distance greater than two have their full
Delaunay stars contained in C: neighbours are at distance at most two,
and convexity puts each triangle there. Thus at least I>=z-B deep
original points are vertices of the complex.

Every boundary edge of this complex has both endpoints in the strip. If
one endpoint were deep, its complete star would supply both incident
triangles. Therefore b<=24B. Every nonempty component has a strip vertex;
otherwise the component would be a finite collection of complete plane
stars, with no boundary edge, which is impossible for a nonempty finite
planar triangle complex. Consequently c<=B. It follows that

`T>=2*I-26*B>=2*z-28*B`.

The contained triangle interiors are disjoint. Each has area at least a
by the unit-side/circumradius argument in the effective-chart report.
Therefore

`Sigma<=Q-a*T<=Q-a_0*z+28*a*B`
`=a_0*S-a_0*(p/2+1)+28*a*B<=a_0*S+28*a*B`.

This is one disjoint area currency. No overlap of nested stresses or
independent charts is used. If there are no contained triangles, the
same estimate remains valid.

## 4. Boundary defects and metric defects

Declare exceptional every strip vertex and every vertex of a triangle
incident to a strip vertex. At most 24B triangles are incident to the
strip, so this declaration costs at most 73B vertices. At all remaining
vertices every incident triangle is wholly contained in C and its
vertices are deep.

For each obtuse-or-right triangle with deep vertices, the neighbour across
its longest edge is contained in C. The local Delaunay pair inequality
in the effective-chart report gives total pair excess at least delta.
Each contained triangle is charged at most four times. Thus the number
of such obtuse triangles is at most 4Sigma/delta. Independently, the
number of contained triangles of excess at least 1/100 is at most
100Sigma. Their incident vertices cost at most
`(12/delta+300)*Sigma`.

At an interior vertex outside these sets all incident triangles are
acute with excess less than 1/100. The side/angle bounds in the effective
chart report hold at this per-triangle scope. If its valence is not six,
angle sum 2pi requires at least pi/150>1/50 total incident excess.
The sum of all vertex-incident excess is at most 3Sigma, giving fewer
than 150Sigma additional exceptional vertices. This proves the stated
bound. Integer counts can be rounded downward after summing, not before
an overlapping charge.

Local equilateral development is available in defect-free simply connected
patches. A global chart through the holes/exceptional vertices is not
claimed: defect holonomy, cuts and orientation matching remain to be
controlled. The diffuse-strain regression shows that even zero exceptional
vertices do not remove the metric budget.

## 5. Exterior exact saturation: a rederived Worker 2 input

Here distinguish the core hull F=conv(V) from C. In the canonical source,
V is the nonisolated positive-contact core. All actual diameter endpoints
belong to V by the promoted ZR/ZF normalization. The unloaded core vertices
are harmonic in each coordinate until reaching loaded vertices, so the
maximum principle gives conv(V)=conv(B), the loaded force hull. No such
claim is made about contact isolates.

The following special case of Worker 2's E.6 is independently rederived,
not promoted by its branch ancestry. Provenance: immutable W2 tip
`f4a362727779f6afc0f01738015112c04fdb73d5`,
`docs/reports/target_c_exterior_packet_release_and_unloaded_core_cut_2026-10-06.md`,
Section 6. Its general multi-site stress-support argument is not needed here.

**Exterior one-site saturation.** There is no point r outside int(F),
distinct from the source, unit-clear (>=1) from every old source point,
with every r-to-old upper distance strictly below D.

Indeed delete any actual contact-active core vertex v and insert r.
Choose a unit supporting/separating direction u at r with
`(r-x) dot u>=0` for every core pin x in V; this exists because
r is outside int(conv V). If outside F use nearest-point separation;
if on its boundary use a supporting normal. Give r velocity u. For
an old isolate z having |r-z|=1 give z velocity u+(z-r); give all other
isolates zero velocity. Retain every isolate and pin all remaining core
vertices. At an r-to-pin unit pair the first half-squared variation is
nonnegative and its relative velocity u is nonzero, so the straight
motion opens it by its first or second positive term. At an r-to-isolate
unit pair the first half-squared variation is exactly one. All old pairs
involving isolates have strict lower and upper slack; every r-involving
upper constraint is strict by hypothesis. A sufficiently small positive
finite amplitude preserves the entire list of inactive constraints and
opens every new contact. All retained-to-retained changed pairs have
been included through the isolate velocities. There is only one inserted
point, hence no inserted-to-inserted pair. Cardinality is unchanged.

The final graph is the induced old graph after deleting v, hence remains
triangle-free and loses at least one actual contact. Every retained
actual diameter pair stays unchanged; those incident to v disappear;
no new diameter pair appears. Diameter below D contradicts globality;
diameter D contradicts contact minimality. This proves the special case.

Every new point in the old hull K=conv(A) has strictly sub-D distances
to every old source point: a nonvertex convex combination gives strict
convexity of squared norm, and the vertices of K are already old points.
Thus in the common saturated completion P, every auxiliary point in K
lies strictly inside F. In particular, no auxiliary point is added in
any closed exterior cap contained in K outside int(F). The generic
Delaunay transfer therefore uses **zero added cap sites** there, even
though it still has O(D) boundary and metric defects.

This conclusion strengthens compatibility of the cap/source skeleton;
it does not manufacture capacity. It agrees with the low-angle zero-slot
barrier and the full exterior-only packet release from W2. It does not
permit a static exterior packet to pay even one deleted core label.
A successful cap endpoint must move core/wall labels, enter int(F), or
control new active upper constraints.

## 6. Remaining quantified bridge

For every sufficiently large canonical source in the macroscopic branch,
use this actual common O(D) skeleton to produce one same-cardinality
finite competitor that either loses contacts, loses an actual diameter
pair at equal contact count, has all upper distances strictly below D,
or forces an exact unit triangle. Required checks include all changed
retained-old pairs, every inserted pair, old actual diameters and inactive
upper exceptions, contacts, diameter count, triangles and cardinality.

Two distinct attacks now use the same data: propagate defect/metric strain
to a removable boundary set, or move a cap-backed/core stress transversal
and refill through the force hull. The full-core face-coupled route remains
independent. This report does not close C-II or Level I; the continuous
closure run proceeds to these finite-move obligations.
